! The corpus stays on disk

The wiki reads `tools/`, `syntaxes/`, `snippets/`, `docs/` and `tests/` from where those files
sit. The corpus stands apart, and this tiddler points at it instead of carrying it.

! Why it points rather than loads

A gate measures the corpus. Every scope the grammar declares should reach some file there, and
`corpus/coverage-floor.txt` ratchets the count so a rule the corpus once exercised cannot quietly
go unexercised. A copy of those files inside the wiki would answer that measurement twice,
and the two copies would part on the day somebody edits one — the gate would go on reading the
files on disk while a reader here looked at the other set and believed it.

The specimens also serve a machine rather than a person. Each one exists for a tokenizer, not for
a reader: `degenerate.*` holds unterminated constructs on purpose, and the containment check appends a
sentence to each file and requires that sentence to carry only what the same sentence carries
alone. Opening one in the wiki shows characters that mean something only to the gate that reads
them.

! Where it sits

|!path |!ground |
|`corpus/wikitext/` |blocks, inline runs, html, pragmas, and two degenerate files |
|`corpus/tid/` |`.tid`, `.meta` and `.multids` files |
|`corpus/memetic/` |sigils, `lar:` URIs, the control set, fences, and a degenerate file |
|`corpus/testfile/` |the control the containment check measures its baseline from |

Four files hold the rulings rather than the ground:

|!path |!what it holds |
|`corpus/coverage-floor.txt` |the count that may rise and may never fall |
|`corpus/unreached-ceiling.txt` |the count that may fall and may never rise |
|`corpus/expected-divergence.txt` |scope prefixes that diverge by ruling, each with its reason |
|`corpus/colour-pair-floor.txt` |the pairs a theme paints apart, ratcheted |

! Reading it

```sh
npm run corpus           # the gate
npm run corpus-verbose   # and every scope nothing reaches
```

`corpus/README.md` carries the same account beside the files, so a reader who arrives through the
directory finds it without arriving here first. `tools/bench.sh` seeds its workspace from the
corpus, so a bench session looks at the same files that gate the grammar.
